<header><p class="st-kicker">other · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Guiyi Circuit</h1><p class="st-meta">848 CE – 1036 CE · capital Dunhuang (Shazhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The self-governing Chinese regime at Dunhuang that threw off Tibetan rule in 848 and whose rulers sponsored many of the Mogao caves and their famous manuscripts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 848, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> disintegrated, the local leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yichao">Zhang Yichao</a> drove the Tibetan garrison out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> (Shazhou) and soon of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> towns. He sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> court, which in 851 recognised him as military governor of the Guiyi ('Return to Allegiance') Army.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Though nominally a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties</a> province, it was a hereditary oasis state, first under the Zhang family and from 914 under the Cao family, who married into the Khotanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> royal houses. Its rulers financed cave temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogao-caves">Mogao</a>, and its monasteries produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in Chinese, Tibetan, Khotanese, Sogdian and Uyghur. Neighbours such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganzhou-uyghur-kingdom">Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom</a> often threatened it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cao rule weakened in the early 11th century, and around 1036 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-xia">Western Xia</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>; the details of the final years are debated. The cave library, sealed around this time, was rediscovered in 1900.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/guiyi-circuit.jpg" alt="Guiyi Circuit" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guiyi_Circuit.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>848 CE – 1036 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dunhuang (Shazhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhang Yichao, Cao Yijin, Cao Yuanzhong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Tibetan, Khotanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guiyi Army, Return to Allegiance Circuit</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=guiyi-circuit" aria-label="Guiyi Circuit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7009 742 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Guiyi Circuit lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7329 998L7771 1192M7329 998L7641 1054"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0M7641 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7329 998h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7329 998h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dunhuang. <a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=guiyi-circuit">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

